---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const title = 'Stablecoin Lifecycle Terms — Stable or Gone Guide';
const description = 'A guide to stablecoin launch, migration, rebrand, wind-down, discontinuation, failure, post-collapse trading, and successor or parallel asset lifecycle terms in Stable or Gone.';
const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  headline: title,
  url: 'https://sog.badjoke-lab.com/guides/stablecoin-lifecycle-terms/',
  description,
  isPartOf: { '@type': 'WebSite', name: 'Stable or Gone', url: 'https://sog.badjoke-lab.com/' },
  about: ['stablecoin lifecycle', 'stablecoin wind-down', 'stablecoin failure', 'stablecoin migration']
};
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} canonicalPath="/guides/stablecoin-lifecycle-terms/" jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="panel hero-main">
      <div class="prompt">&gt; guide --lifecycle</div>
      <h1>Stablecoin Lifecycle Terms</h1>
      <p>SOG records stablecoins as historical entities. A token can launch, migrate, rebrand, wind down, fail, or continue trading after failure in a different lifecycle context.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="panel registry">
    <div class="bar">why lifecycle terms matter</div>
    <p>
      Stablecoins do not only move between active and failed. Some keep operating with limits, some stop minting, some enter a planned wind-down, some migrate to a new token or protocol design, and some keep trading after the original stablecoin function has failed. SOG uses lifecycle language to avoid mixing these different outcomes into one vague label.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="panel registry">
    <div class="bar">terms</div>
    <table><tbody>
      <tr><th>launch</th><td>The initial release or public availability of a stablecoin or protocol asset.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>migration</th><td>A move to a new token, contract, chain, issuer structure, or protocol design.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>rebrand</th><td>A name or brand change that may or may not involve a technical migration.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>wind-down</th><td>A planned or announced process to end issuance, support, redemption, conversion, or exchange availability.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>discontinued</th><td>A lifecycle state where issuance or support has ended, without necessarily implying a depeg collapse.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>failure</th><td>A severe lifecycle outcome where the stablecoin did not return as a functioning stablecoin.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>post-collapse trading</th><td>Market trading after failure. This is not the same as restored peg or recovered stablecoin status.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>successor or parallel asset</th><td>A related token or product that may coexist with or replace an earlier stablecoin, such as protocol migration or ecosystem transition context.</td></tr>
    </tbody></table>
  </section>

  <section class="panel registry">
    <div class="bar">examples in SOG</div>
    <table><tbody>
      <tr><th>UST</th><td>Failed status is tied to the May 2022 collapse. USTC post-collapse trading is treated separately.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>BUSD</th><td>Discontinued status is tied to issuer wind-down, not a depeg collapse.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>DAI</th><td>DAI and Sky-related successor or parallel asset context needs source-backed lifecycle handling.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>LUSD</th><td>LUSD V1 and Liquity V2/BOLD context should be separated rather than merged into one vague record.</td></tr>
    </tbody></table>
  </section>

  <section class="section-grid" aria-label="Lifecycle examples">
    <a class="panel mini-card" href="/stablecoin/ust/"><h2>UST / TerraUSD</h2><p>Failed lifecycle outcome with post-collapse USTC separated.</p></a>
    <a class="panel mini-card" href="/stablecoin/busd/"><h2>BUSD</h2><p>Issuer wind-down and discontinued status, not collapse framing.</p></a>
    <a class="panel mini-card" href="/stablecoin/dai/"><h2>DAI</h2><p>Protocol lifecycle, Sky transition, and successor or parallel asset context.</p></a>
  </section>

  <section class="panel registry">
    <div class="bar">related</div>
    <table><tbody>
      <tr><th>Read next</th><td><a href="/guides/status-vs-event/">Status vs Event</a> · <a href="/guides/what-is-a-depeg/">What is a Depeg?</a> · <a href="/glossary/">Glossary</a> · <a href="/events/">Events</a></td></tr>
    </tbody></table>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
